      "id": "the-memory-book-ch-12",
      "order": 12,
      "chapterNumber": 12,
      "headline": "Decoding Prices, Style Codes, and Phone Numbers",
      "keyInsight": "Modern life bombards us with stubborn mixed codes: product style numbers, price tags, and seven-digit telephone numbers. Most people write them down on scraps of paper or repeat them five times before forgetting them anyway. The hidden trap is that these codes seem completely disconnected from real life. A phone number or a suit price feels like noise. But once you realize letters and digits speak the exact same language, the noise vanishes. A product code like 410 becomes a 'rose rod'. A price like eighty-four dollars becomes 'fire'. By gluing that funny word to the actual item, the price tag sticks to the product forever.",
      "content": "Think of price tags and style codes like price stickers on fresh produce at the supermarket. Paper stickers peel off, smudge, and get lost in the grocery bag. To remember a price or a style number, you must bake the number right into the item itself. If a sleek leather jacket costs ninety-five dollars, you convert ninety-five into the word 'bell'. Now visualize ringing a giant, heavy brass bell right inside the jacket pocket. The cost is no longer a separate piece of paper. It is an inseparable part of the jacket.\n\nStyle numbers that combine letters and digits work the exact same way. If a car battery part is 804-B, you turn 804 into 'visor' and add a word for B, like 'bee'. Picture an angry bee buzzing around behind the car sun visor. In an instant, a confusing string of letters and numbers becomes a quick cartoon scenario you could spot across a crowded room.\n\nTelephone numbers simply combine exchange names with a two-object action scene. You attach the person's face to the exchange word, then let that word crash into the remaining digits. When you look at their face, the whole story unspools. You will never need to scramble for a misplaced pen or phone book again.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Attach prices and codes directly to the object by converting raw digits into vivid, interacting pictures.",
      "audioSnippetText": "Paper price tags fall off, and random phone numbers slip away like water. Turn prices and codes into vivid pictures that you weld directly onto the item or person.",
